Open libunwind's address-validation pipe under a lock.

To check that an address is readable, libunwind writes one byte from it into
a pipe. Since v1.8.0 (commit ec03043244082b8f552881ba9fb790aa49c85468,
"Simplified address validation") it opens that pipe on first use behind an
atomic flag. The flag only says that some thread has started to open the
pipe. A second thread that validates an address in that window finds the flag
set and no descriptors, takes the "re-open closed pipe" branch, and the two
threads then close and replace each other's descriptors. A write fails, the
address is reported invalid and unw_step() returns an error for a frame that
is fine, or the process gets SIGPIPE (libunwind/libunwind#715). The .NET
runtime treats the failed step as fatal (dotnet/runtime#130577, #134044).

With this patch the first opening happens under a lock, and the flag is set
only when the pipe is open. The lock is taken the way libunwind takes its
other locks, with all signals blocked (lock_acquire()). tdep_init() does the
same for the library's own initialisation, and 1.7 opened this pipe there. So:

- A thread that needs the pipe while another thread opens it waits for that
  thread.
- A signal handler that unwinds cannot interrupt the thread that holds the
  lock, so unw_step() stays safe in a signal handler, as unw_step(3) promises.
  Opening the pipe under pthread_once() without blocking signals, as the copy
  of libunwind inside the .NET runtime does (dotnet/runtime#97817), is not: the
  handler would wait for the thread it interrupted.
- When pipe2() fails because no descriptor is free, the flag stays clear and
  the next caller tries again, under the lock.

Nothing else changes. The "re-open closed pipe" branch is as it was. A child
that is forked while another thread holds this lock inherits it locked, as it
does libunwind's other locks.

Upstream fixed the first-use race on master by giving every thread a pipe of
its own (libunwind/libunwind#993, commit 346fefe1845c, in no release). That
pipe is never closed, so a process leaks two descriptors for every thread
that unwinds and exits. This patch keeps the single pipe of v1.8.3.

--- a/src/mi/Gaddress_validator.c
+++ b/src/mi/Gaddress_validator.c
@@ -38,7 +38,8 @@
 #include <stdatomic.h>
 
 
-static atomic_flag _unw_address_validator_initialized = ATOMIC_FLAG_INIT;
+static atomic_bool _unw_address_validator_initialized = false;
+static define_lock (_unw_address_validator_lock);
 static int _mem_validate_pipe[2] = {-1, -1};
 
 #ifdef HAVE_PIPE2
@@ -104,9 +105,27 @@
   int ret = -1;
   ssize_t bytes = 0;
 
-  if (unlikely (!atomic_flag_test_and_set(&_unw_address_validator_initialized)))
+  if (unlikely (!atomic_load (&_unw_address_validator_initialized)))
     {
-      if (_open_pipe () != 0)
+      intrmask_t saved_mask;
+      int status = 0;
+
+      /* Open the pipe under a lock, and say that it is open only when it is:
+       * a thread that needs the pipe while another thread is opening it waits
+       * for that thread instead of using descriptors that are not there yet.
+       * Like libunwind's other locks, this one is held with all signals
+       * blocked, so a signal handler that unwinds cannot interrupt the thread
+       * that holds it. If the pipe cannot be opened, the next caller tries
+       * again. */
+      lock_acquire (&_unw_address_validator_lock, saved_mask);
+      if (!atomic_load (&_unw_address_validator_initialized))
+        {
+          status = _open_pipe ();
+          if (status == 0)
+            atomic_store (&_unw_address_validator_initialized, true);
+        }
+      lock_release (&_unw_address_validator_lock, saved_mask);
+      if (status != 0)
         {
           return false;
         }
